    "title": "$117.5M Comcast data breach settlement: Who qualifies and how to file a claim",
    "text": "Patrick De Haan, a GasBuddy petroleum analyst, said the increase will likely be noticeable, but not immediate or as severe as past spikes. SACRAMENTO, Calif. — Drivers across the country are bracing for higher gas prices as rising tensions between the United States and Iran rattle global oil markets. Crude prices have already jumped, and analysts warn that increase is beginning to show up at the pump. If the conflict continues to escalate, they say this could be only the start. On Monday, drivers lined up at gas stations before prices are set to rise. \"For a while there, they were going down noticeably, and now they're notably creeping back up. I think that has something to do with what's going on in the world currently,\" said Danielle Grossman, a driver filling up her tank. According to the Associated Press, U.S. oil traded 7.6% higher at $72.12 per barrel, while international standard Brent crude rose 8.6% to $79.11 per barrel. Steve Swatt, a political analyst, said the economic impact is already being felt following the U.S.-Israeli conflict with Iran. \"Well, economically, you're going to see the price of gasoline rising, and already some experts are saying expect it to go up at least 30 cents a gallon in the near future. And so that's another thing that will play on the public's mind here domestically,\" Swatt said. For some drivers, avoiding the increase isn’t an option. Maureen Dillie said she fills up every four to five days. \"Well, I have 5 children and so I drive a lot. I average about 26,000 miles a year. And then because I was driving about 240 miles a day, an electric vehicle wasn't something that was feasible, so I stuck with this, and I use a lot of gas,\" Dillie said. Patrick De Haan, a GasBuddy petroleum analyst, said the increase will likely be noticeable, but not immediate or as severe as past spikes. \"This is not going to be an overnight massive spike, but it will be noticeable to most motorists. But again, to put this in comparison to Russia, this is not going to be nearly what we saw back in 2022 when not only Russia invaded Ukraine, but when we saw COVID relief as well,\" De Haan said. Families say they now have little choice but to adjust their budgets. \"In my personal opinion, it's unnecessarily being thrown upon us, I guess, to say. It's just politics, and consumers are paying the price for that, and it's unfortunate,\" Grossman said. Swatt questioned how long public support might last if the conflict drags on. \"I think the longer this goes on, the more you have perhaps retaliations against our country one way or another. You have oil going up. I think support for this will probably erode,\" he said. Drivers say when conflicts between countries stretch on for months or even years, gas prices tend to follow. For now, many expect costs at the pump to keep climbing for the foreseeable future. WATCH ALSO: ABC10: Watch, Download, Read",
